# Ground truth for Contrast Security's go-test-bench, a deliberate
# vulnerability benchmark.
#
# Every expectation below comes from the benchmark's own declaration of what it
# demonstrates, not from what either engine currently reports. Its OpenAPI
# document (cmd/go-swagger/restapi/embedded_spec.go) enumerates the routes as
# "/<family>/<vulnerable function>/query/{safety}", and each one names the
# function the payload is supposed to reach.
#
# Until this file existed, no real repository in the manifest carried ground
# truth, so recall and precision were only ever measured on the ninety-odd
# twenty-line corpus fixtures. On this repository the two engines reported 62
# and 17 findings and nothing in the harness could say which was closer to
# right.
#
# Every route takes its payload from an HTTP query parameter, so the source is
# http-input throughout. Each expectation is pinned to the sink function the
# benchmark names, so a flow reaching some other sink cannot satisfy it.
#
# The {safety} segment selects a sanitized or unsanitized variant of the same
# route, but both live in one binary and reach the same sink, so a want-not on
# the sanitized path is not expressible per-route here. Sanitizer behaviour is
# covered by the corpus instead.

# Both engines miss almost all of this, and for one shared reason. Every route
# reaches its vulnerable function the same way: internal/common/handler.go:42
# calls s.VulnerableFnWrapper(opaque, payload), where VulnerableFnWrapper is a
# func-typed *struct field* holding a closure that the route registered. Neither
# engine carries taint through a function value stored in a field and invoked
# indirectly, so the payload is lost at that one line and nothing downstream —
# exec.Command, os.ReadFile, http.Get, db.Exec — is ever reached from the
# request. That is defect 31, and it is the single largest known cause of the
# gap between what these engines find on twenty-line fixtures and what they
# find on real code.
#
# SEAM finds all nine. Getting there took five separate fixes, none of which
# the corpus could have prompted: resolving dispatch through a func-typed
# struct field at all; not minting source labels during summary computation,
# which replaced the param labels the ParamReturn matcher keys on; naming the
# summary-derived sink rather than the dispatch temp; reading taint out of a
# variadic slice when applying a passthrough, without which fmt.Sprintf — the
# commonest taint carrier in Go — returned clean; and unioning the result taint
# over every candidate a field may hold rather than the first.
#
# Legacy finds one, so the other eight carry known-fail=legacy:31 rather than
# no marker: dropping a marker because the candidate passes leaves the other
# engine failing with nothing recording why.

# Command injection: exec.Command and exec.CommandContext.
want flow source=http-input sink=command-execution sinkfn=~os/exec.Command known-fail=legacy:31
want flow source=http-input sink=command-execution sinkfn=~os/exec.CommandContext known-fail=legacy:31

# Path traversal: four separate filesystem entry points.
want flow source=http-input sink=filesystem sinkfn=~os.ReadFile known-fail=legacy:31
want flow source=http-input sink=filesystem sinkfn=~os.Open known-fail=legacy:31
want flow source=http-input sink=filesystem sinkfn=~os.WriteFile known-fail=legacy:31
want flow source=http-input sink=filesystem sinkfn=~os.Create known-fail=legacy:31

# SQL injection: the payload is concatenated into a statement run by the
# sqlite3 driver. Pinned to the sink, because an unpinned "sink=data" was
# satisfied by an unrelated encoding/json.Unmarshal flow elsewhere in the
# program and reported recall the engine had not earned.
want flow source=http-input sink=data sinkfn=~Exec known-fail=legacy:31

# SSRF: the payload becomes the URL passed to http.Get.
want flow source=http-input sink=external-service sinkfn=~http.Get known-fail=legacy:31

# Reflected XSS: the payload is echoed into the response body. Under the gin
# server that is Context.String; the same route exists on the other frameworks.
want flow source=http-input sink=http-response
